  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nSprawling surface high pressure has resulted in another morning\nof clear skies amid a very dry lower atmosphere. This has\nallowed temperatures to quickly fall into the 30s and low 40s \nacross much of the area, amid some developing river valley steam\nfog. Expect a slow increase in fog coverage, but overall, \nshould be much less extensive than that of yesterday morning. \nLows this morning will be similar to that of yesterday morning,\nperhaps a touch colder in some locations given less fog \ncoverage, with low/mid 30s expected across much of the area. \nPatchy sub-freezing temperatures are expected across the \ntypical valley cold spots away from lakes and rivers. A Frost \nAdvisory remains in effect until 9 AM for all zones where the \ngrowing season is still active.\n\nA persistence pattern will be in place over the next few days as\nhigh pressure continues to prevail across the region. Soundings\ndepict a rather dry layer near H850, which will likely mix down\nto an extent during the afternoons. Given such, have lowered\nafternoon dew points over the next few days, with minimum\nafternoon RH values generally in the 30s, but amid very light\nsurface flow. Seasonable daytime temperatures are expected, \nwith mid to upper 60s across the lowlands amid mostly sunny \nskies.\n\nThe rather dry low/mid levels will continue to facilitate very\nefficient radiational cooling throughout the work week. Given\nsuch, have lowered overnight temperatures a few degrees from\ncentral guidance, with widespread low temperatures in the 30s \nexpected through Thursday morning. Do anticipate the potential \nneed for frost headlines tonight (and perhaps additional \nnights) given low/mid 30s in some areas, but will hold off for \nthe time being and allow day shift to re-evaluate. Slightly \nwarmer overnight temperatures are expected late in the work week\ninto the weekend given developing return flow, as extended dry \nweather continues area-wide. \n\nOvernight and early morning river valley steam fog is expected\nthe next few days given a continued large air / water \ntemperature difference, with fog locally dense in spots. A \ngradual reduction in coverage is anticipated each night however.",
